#: ../../../projectconf/sections/env/index.rst:80
msgid "Working ``[env:NAME]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projectconf/sections/env/index.rst:82
msgid ""
"A working environment in PlatformIO is defined using the ``[env:NAME]`` "
"syntax. This environment is used by various PlatformIO commands such as :ref:"
"`cmd_run`, :ref:`cmd_test`, :ref:`cmd_check`, :ref:`cmd_debug`, and others. "
"Every project must define at least one working environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projectconf/sections/env/index.rst:87
msgid ""
"You can define multiple working environments in your project by using "
"different names for the ``[env:NAME]`` section. It's important to note that "
"each environment must have a unique name. The name should only include "
"lowercase letters ``a-z``, numbers ``0-9``, and special characters ``_`` "
"(underscore) and ``-`` (hyphen). For example, ``[env:hello_world]`` is a "
"valid name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projectconf/sections/env/index.rst:93
msgid ""
"If you have defined multiple working environments and you only want to "
"process a subset of them, you can use the ``-e`` or ``--environment`` option "
"with the PlatformIO commands mentioned above. You can specify the name of "
"the environment you want to process using this option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../projectconf/sections/env/index.rst:98
msgid ""
"In addition to using the ``-e`` option, you can define a default set of "
"working environments for the PlatformIO commands to process by using the "
"``[platformio]`` :ref:`projectconf_pio_default_envs` option. This can be "
"useful if you frequently work with a specific set of environments and don't "
"want to specify them every time you run a command."
msgstr ""

